<h1>Aqueous Archive Format</h1>
<pre class=metadata>
Shortname: aqueous
Status: LS
Boilerplate: omit feedback-header
Editor: Kang Seonghoon, https://mearie.org/
Repository: TODO
Abstract: This specification defines the Aqueous archive format, a compressed archive format designed for efficient storage and retrieval of files.
Markup Shorthands: markdown yes
</pre>

<style>
td:has(+td>a[href="#type-offset"])>dfn {
  &::before { content: "("; }
  &::after { content: ")"; }
}
a[href="#type-offset"] {
  font-style: italic;
}
</style>

<h2 id=definitions>
Definitions</h2>

<dl>
  <dt><dfn export>Archive</dfn> or <dfn export>Uncompressed Archive</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A logical collection of files, directories, and associated metadata.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Barely Compressible</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A condition where the algorithm overhead from compression exceeds the size saving
  so that the normal Compressed Block can't be used.

  <div class=note>
    By definition, this condition varies by the choice of compression algorithm.
    In the case of Zstandard, the minimum overhead is 3 bytes per block of at most 2<sup>21</sup>-1 bytes.
  </div>
  </dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Bit</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The smallest unit of information, representing a binary value of either 0 or 1.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Byte</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A sequence of 8 bits, typically the smallest addressable unit of data.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compress</dfn> or <dfn export>Compression</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The process of transforming an uncompressed archive into a compressed archive, typically to reduce its size.
  This is the inverse operation of decompression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compressor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A component or algorithm responsible for performing compression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compressed Archive</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A sequence of bytes that represents an archive, organized in a way that is usually, but not necessarily,
  more compact than the naive concatenation of its constituent parts.
  A single uncompressed archive can have multiple valid compressed representations.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Decompress</dfn> or <dfn export>Decompression</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The process of transforming a compressed archive back into its original uncompressed form.
  This is the inverse operation of compression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Decompressor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A component or algorithm responsible for performing decompression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Dictionary</dfn></dt>
  <dd>TBW</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Directory</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A special type of file that contains references to other files and/or directories,
  forming a hierarchical file system structure.
  It includes its own name and associated metadata.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>File</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A fundamental unit of archival, consisting of a sequence of zero or more content bytes,
  a non-empty byte sequence for its name, and any other associated metadata (e.g., size, modification time, permissions).</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Implicitly-sized</dfn></dt>
  <dd>Being its byte length only available through the context.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Postprocess</dfn> or <dfn export>Postprocessor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The act or component that performs operations on data *after* it has been decompressed or transformed,
  but before it is considered fully restored to its original state.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Preprocess</dfn> or <dfn export>Preprocessor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The act or component that performs operations on data *before* it is compressed or transformed,
  but after it has been retrieved from its original source.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Transform</dfn> or <dfn export>Transformation</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A combined term for matching preprocessing and postprocessing algorithms.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Transformer</dfn></dt>
  <dd>An algorithm responsible for performing transformation.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Zstandard block</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The "Block" structure in the Zstandard frame [[RFC8878]], Section 3.1.1.2.</dd>
</dl>

<h2 id=conventions>
Conventions</h2>

<h3 id=expressions>
Expressions</h3>

All expressions are interpreted according to the standard arithmetics, with the following extensions:

*   |a| / |b| equals to floor(|a| ÷ |b|).
*   |a| % |b| is a non-negative modulo of |a| divided by |b|, and equals to |a| - (|a| / |b|) × |b|.
*   |a| &lt;&lt; |b| equals to |a| × 2<sup>|b|</sup>.
*   |a| &gt;&gt; |b| equals to |a| / 2<sup>|b|</sup>.
*   |XX|<sub>16</sub> denotes a hexadecimal integer.
*   (|a|, |b|, …) denotes an ordered array of values.
*   (|XX| |YY| |ZZ|)<sub>16</sub> is a shorthand for (|XX|<sub>16</sub>, |YY|<sub>16</sub>, |ZZ|<sub>16</sub>).
*   |a| || |b| concatenates two arrays |a| and |b|, treating any non-array value as a singleton array (|a|) or (|b|).
*   For array |a|, |a|<sup>|n|</sup> equals to |a| || |a|<sup>|n|-1</sup> when |n| > 0.
    |a|<sup>0</sup> equals to an empty array ().
*   [|a|, |b|] denotes a closed interval between |a| and |b|, both inclusive.
*   [|a|, |b|) denotes a half-open interval between |a| (inclusive) and |b| (exclusive).

All expressions result in an integer between -(2<sup>64</sup> - 1) and 2<sup>64</sup> - 1,
or an array of those integers with the number of elements not exceeding 2<sup>64</sup> - 1.
Note that subexpressions still may exceed this range.

<h3 id=types>
Types</h3>

Types are a set of values and associated encoding scheme.
Unless stated as being [=implicitly-sized=],
all types have the defined byte length out of the context.

<dfn for=type>bool</dfn> is a Boolean value, either true or false.
It has no associated encoding scheme and can't occur in any structure.

<dfn for=type>u8</dfn> is an unsigned 8-bit integer, occupying 1 byte.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>8</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u16le</dfn> is an unsigned 16-bit integer, occupying 2 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order (least significant byte first).
Its range is [0, 2<sup>16</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u32le</dfn> is an unsigned 32-bit integer, occupying 4 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>32</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u64le</dfn> is an unsigned 64-bit integer, occupying 8 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<strong><dfn for=type>u</dfn>(|n|)</strong>, where |n| is an integer less than 2<sup>64</sup>,
is the smallest integer type that can represent |n| if |n| > 0,
and a zero-sized type if |n| = 0.
In the other words,

* If |n| = 0, u(|n|) is zero-sized.
* If |n| ∈ [1, 2<sup>8</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u8=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>8</sup>, 2<sup>16</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u16le=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>16</sup>, 2<sup>32</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u32le=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>32</sup>, 2<sup>64</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u64le=].

<dfn for=type>leb128</dfn> is an unsigned 64-bit integer encoded using the variable number of bytes with the LEB128 encoding.
Each byte, except the last, has its most significant bit (MSB) set to 1, indicating that more bytes follow.
The remaining 7 bits of each byte are used to store the integer's value.
The least significant 7 bits of the first byte are the least significant bits of the integer, and so on.
The last byte has its MSB set to 0.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<div class=note>
  This allows for multiple encodings for given number for any number smaller than 2<sup>32</sup>.
</div>

<strong>|a|[|n|]</strong>, where |n| is an integer, denotes an ordered array of *a*s.
|n|'s range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<strong>|a|[*]</strong> denotes an [=implicitly-sized=] unordered array of *a*s with the unspecified quantity.
The number of elements is implicitly determined from the context.
This specification uses two kinds of such contexts:

*   When one or more |a|[*] fields are in the structure with the defined byte length,
    the numbers of elements in those fields are increased as needed to exactly fill the required byte length.
    There MUST be a single set of the numbers of elements that satisfy these conditions.
*   When an |a|[*] field is given with a constraint,
    the field extends until the first instance of |a| which doesn't satisfy the required constraint.

<dfn for=type>string</dfn> is an alias of [=u8=][*] with an additional constraint that
the byte sequence is a valid UTF-8 string. [[RFC3629]]

<h3 id=structure>
Structure</h3>

**Structure** is a logical group of one or more ordered **fields** of possibly differing types concatenated together.
Each field has the following properties:

* The descriptive **name** of the field (e.g. [=block/size=], [=cdata/compression_params=]).
* The data **type** of the field (e.g. [=leb128=], [=u8=][size]), with an optional constraint.
* Optional criteria or **conditions** that dictate the presence, value, or interpretation of the field.

Field names can only coincide when the field's type varies by conditions,
in which case at most one such condition is applicable at any time.
The name can be omitted when the field's name is same as the previous field.

The field type may be <dfn for=type>offset</dfn>,
which is a special zero-sized type interpreted as
the offset to the following byte relative to the unspecified file position (origin).
Offsets are only used to measure the number of bytes within a set of fields.
The syntax and semantics of this specification don't depend on the choice of origin.

The optional constraint does not affect the field's encoding and only narrows down the possible set of values. Constraints can be one of the following:

*   |a| ∈ |X| requires that the value is in the specified set |X|.
*   |a| ∉ |X| requires that the value is not in the specified set |X|.
*   |a| = |x| requires that the value is exactly the given value |x|.
    If |x| is a constant expression, this effectively fixes the byte encoding of this field.
*   |a| ≠ |x| requires that the value is not the given value |x|.

The conditions may be one of the following:

* "<dfn for=cond>Optional</dfn>" indicates that conforming implementations MAY include or exclude given field as needed.
* "<dfn for=cond>If</dfn> …" indicates that the field MUST be included only when the specified condition is true.
* "<dfn for=cond>Depends on</dfn> …" indicates that the field's interpretation varies with the specified set of other fields.

Structures are [=implicitly-sized=] only when its byte length is not available through the context
and at least one [=implicitly-sized=] field exists therein.

<h3 id=constants>
Constants</h3>

This specification defines and uses the following constants, all of which are denoted in UPPERCASE.

<table class=data dfn-for=const>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Value
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>HEADER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>278<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>FOOTER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>98&thinsp;68EB&thinsp;D5FD<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>15D&thinsp;14FC&thinsp;E5F9<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>HEADER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(F8&nbsp;04&nbsp;03&nbsp;6A&nbsp;D6&nbsp;BE)<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>FOOTER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(FD&nbsp;AA&nbsp;AE&nbsp;DE&nbsp;85&nbsp;12)<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(F9&nbsp;CA&nbsp;F2&nbsp;A6&nbsp;D0&nbsp;2A&nbsp;01&nbsp;00)<sub>16</sub>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  The shortest LEB128 encoding of each |XXX_TYPE| is a prefix of each |XXX_MAGIC| byte sequence.
</div>

<h2 id=syntax>
Syntax</h2>

<h3 id=block>
Block</h3>

**Blocks** are a typed byte sequence.
The entirety of an unencrypted archive and the beginning of an encrypted archive consists of either blocks or block-like structures.

Blocks have the following generic structure:

<div dfn-for=block>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>type</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ [=FOOTER_TYPE=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 0
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u16le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 1
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u32le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 2
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u64le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 3
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=block/size=]]
      <td>[=Depends on=] [=block/type=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>padding</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
      <td>[=If=] ([=block_end=] - [=block_start=]) % 2 = 1
  </tbody>
</table>

Some [=block/type=] values, including [=FOOTER_TYPE=], are also used to denote block-like structures.
For example the archive footer has to have a distinct block type in order to be placed after blocks.
This specification doesn't assign any meaning to such block-like structures unless explicitly stated.

The presence of [=block/padding=] ensures that there are even number of bytes in any block.

The following sections describe the contents of [=block/data=] field according to each defined [=block/type=].
Since [=block/type=] also determines the byte length of [=block/size=],
there are generally multiple [=block/type=] values assigned for each logical block type.

</div>

<h4 id=shared-external-structure>
Shared external structure</h4>

Many block types share the same external structure, only varying its internal structure.
It may or may not have been compressed
and may use different <dfn for=block>compression_method</dfn> or <dfn for=block>checksum_method</dfn>
specified by the parent structure.
Each [=checksum_method=] also has an associated, fixed <dfn for=block>checksum_size</dfn>.

The compressed external structure (<dfn for=block>compressed_data</dfn>) consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=cdata>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size_delta</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compression_params</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>[=Depends on=] [=compression_method=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None, [=depends on=] [=checksum_method=]
  </tbody>
</table>

The compressed size (<dfn>compressed_size</dfn>) is inferred from the block size:
[=cdata/compressed_size=] = [=cdata/data_end=] - [=cdata/data_start=].
The [=cdata/original_size_delta=] field encodes the original size
<dfn>original_size</dfn> = [=cdata/compressed_size=] + [=cdata/original_size_delta=].
Therefore [=cdata/compressed_size=] MUST be equal to or less than [=cdata/original_size=];
any [=barely compressible=] block data MUST NOT use compression.

</div>

The uncompressed external structure (<dfn for=block>uncompressed_data</dfn>) consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=udata>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None
  </tbody>
</table>

</div>

<h5 id=zstandard-compression-method>
Zstandard compression method</h5>

This specification only defines a single compression method, Zstandard [[RFC8878]].
Its parameters ([=cdata/compression_params=]) are as follows:

<div dfn-for=zstd>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>window_descriptor</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

The meaning of the [=window_descriptor=] field is identical to the Zstandard's `Window_Descriptor` byte [[RFC9659]].

TBW

</div>

Each data compressed by this method is a sequence of Zstandard blocks,
without an additional framing provided by Zstandard frames.
Conforming decompressors MUST process those blocks consecutively until the final block is signalled by the block header,
at which point all compressed bytes should have been consumed.

<h5 id=xxh64-checksum-method>
XXH64 checksum method</h5>

This specification only defines a single checksum method, XXH64 [[XXHASH]].
Its seed MUST be zero and the result should be stored as [=u64le=].
The resulting [=checksum_size=] is 8.

<h4 id=spacer-block>
Spacer block</h4>

Blocks 0<sub>16</sub> through 3<sub>16</sub> are **spacer blocks**.
Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=spacer>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>space</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=block/size=]] = (0)<sup>[=block/size=]</sup>
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

</div>

Spacer blocks can skip any number of bytes besides from a single byte,
which is not required due to the presence of blockwise [=block/padding=].

The skipped bytes MUST be filled with zero bytes in order to avoid data leak.
Conforming compressors are strongly RECOMMENDED to utilize sparse files for performance.

<h4 id=size-hint-block>
Size hint block</h4>

Block 4<sub>16</sub> is a **size hint block**.
Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=szhint>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ 0
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

If this block is present, it MUST be the first block after the header
and its [=szhint/size_hint=] field SHOULD equal to the footer's [=archive/size_hint=] field.

</div>

<h4 id=metadata-block>
Metadata block</h4>

Blocks 10<sub>16</sub> through 1F<sub>16</sub> are **metadata blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
where each parameter is determined as follows:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>[=compression_method=]
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>10<sub>16</sub> — 13<sub>16</sub>
      <td>None
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>14<sub>16</sub> — 17<sub>16</sub>
      <td>None
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>18<sub>16</sub> — 1B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>1C<sub>16</sub> — 1F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0001&thinsp;|m||c||ss|</code> where:

    * |m| indicates the compression method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]].
    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<div dfn-for=meta>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>previous_metadata_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_transformers</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_dictionaries</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_directories</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_files</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=meta/entries_end=] - [=meta/entries_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>optional_metadata_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>transformer_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_transformers=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_dictionaries=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_parents</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_directories=])[[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] - 1]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_params</dfn>
      <td>[=meta/file_param=][[=meta/num_files=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/entries_size=])[[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] - 1]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries</dfn>
      <td>[=meta/entry=][[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>optional_metadata</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=meta/optional_metadata_size=]]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

All relative offsets are measured from the beginning of the current metadata block,
implying that the offset of zero is invalid.
All valid relative offsets are also even.

Each metadata block describes compressed or uncompressed blocks appeared after the previous metadata block, if any,
and until the current metadata block.
The previous metadata block is linked via [=meta/previous_metadata_offset=]
which is also the maximum possible offsets that can occur in this particular metadata block.
If there is no prior metadata block,
[=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] is the relative offset
to the end of [=archive/header_magic=] in the Unencrypted Archive structure ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
plus 1 so that it never points to a valid block.

Each transformer and dictionary appearing within these offsets is numbered from 1, respectively.
[=meta/num_transformers=] and [=meta/num_dictionaries=] are therefore
the maximum possible value for transformer and dictionary indices.
Relative offsets to corresponding transformer and dictionary blocks are
later recorded as [=meta/transformer_offsets=] and [=meta/dictionary_offsets=] respectively.

The metadata block represents zero or more files and zero or more directories,
collectively called [=meta/entries=] and numbered together.
The root directory is numbered 0,
subsequent directories are numbered from 1 to [=meta/num_directories=],
and individual files are numbered from ([=meta/num_directories=] + 1) to ([=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=]).
Directories can't form a cycle: a parent of the entry index |k| MUST be less than |k| in [=meta/entry_parents=].
The parent of the root directory (entry 0) and the entry 1 is always fixed to None and the root directory,
so they are omitted.

File parameters (<dfn>file_param</dfn>) occur for each file (but not directory), and consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_transformer</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_transformers=])
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_dictionary</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_dictionaries=])
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

Since transformers and dictionaries are numbered from 1,
the zero index is used to indicate the absence of
transformer or dictionary in [=meta/file_transformer=] and [=meta/file_dictionary=].
A relative offset to the corresponding compressed or uncompressed block is recorded as [=meta/file_offset=],
which can't be zero.

Entry structure (<dfn>entry</dfn>) then occurs for each directory and file, in this order, and consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_name</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*] ≠ 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_name_sentinel</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a file
  </tbody>
</table>

Each entry is named according to [=meta/entry_name=].
No name can contain a zero byte, and there are two allowed encodings:

* WTF-8 encoding [[WTF-8]], which is an extension to UTF-8 and losslessly encodes a lone surrogate pair in UTF-16; or
* Byte C1<sub>16</sub> followed by the raw byte sequence.

  <div class=note>
    Byte C1<sub>16</sub> was chosen because it can't occur in WTF-8 and any other known extension to UTF-8.
  </div>

Conforming compressors are strongly RECOMMENDED to use the WTF-8 encoding,
and SHOULD only use the raw byte sequence as a round-trip measure.

The [=meta/entries=] array is the only part that has the unspecified number of bytes,
which is therefore available as [=meta/entries_size=] beforehand.
Any other field can be randomly accessed after reading the initial set of [=leb128=] fields.

</div>

<h5 id=optional-metadata>
Optional metadata</h5>

The remaining bytes are reserved for the **optional metadata**, encoded in CBOR [[RFC8949]].
This part encodes any metadata that is not strictly necessary to read the file,
including associated dates and times, access control information and comments.

The optional metadata is described by the following CDDL [[RFC8610]] definitions:

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-opt-metadata = { $$aqr-opt-metadata }

aqr-offset = uint .gt 0

aqr-text =
  tstr /          ; Unicode
  #6.273(bstr) /  ; UTF-16 + surrogate compat
  bstr            ; In unknown encoding

aqr-time =
  $aqr-time /               ; UTC timestamp or its scaling
  [$aqr-time] /             ; In unknown time zone, encoded like UTC (*)
  [$aqr-time, offset: int]  ; In a known time zone, offset from UTC in 15 minutes ($)

$aqr-time /= #6.100(int)  ; 1d (FAT atime*)
$aqr-time /= #6.121(int)  ; 2s (FAT mtime*, ZIP*, CAB*)
$aqr-time /= int          ; 1s (~ext3, HFS/HFS+, tar)
$aqr-time /= #6.122(int)  ; 10ms (FAT ctime*, ISO 9660$, exFAT$)
$aqr-time /= #6.123(int)  ; 1ms (UDF)
$aqr-time /= #6.124(int)  ; 1us
$aqr-time /= #6.125(int)  ; 100ns (NTFS, RAR*, 7z)
$aqr-time /= #6.126(int)  ; 1ns (ext4, ZFS, Btrfs, APFS)

$$aqr-opt-metadata //= (
  ? &(archive-comment: -1) => aqr-text
  &(entry-extras: -2) => [* aqr-entry-extra]
)

aqr-entry-extra = (
  ? &(crtime: -1) => aqr-time
  ? &(ctime: -2) => aqr-time
  ? &(mtime: -3) => aqr-time
  ? &(atime: -4) => aqr-time
  ? &(arctime: -5) => aqr-time

  ? &(comment: -6) => aqr-text

  ? &(unix-mode: -70) => uint
  ? &(unix-uid: -71) => uint
  ? &(unix-gid: -72) => uint

  ; TODO:
  ; NTFS attributes (ZIP extra 0x000a)
  ; UNIX attributes (ZIP extra 0x000d) - symlink target
  ; other extras for round trip

  ? &(zip-extras: -100) => aqr-zip-extras
)

aqr-zip-extras = { * aqr-zip-extra-id => bstr }
;aqr-zip-extra-id = 0..0xffff
; TODO: should exclude or at least discourage the following:
;       0x0001 - ZIP64
;       0x5455 - extended timestamp
;       0x7075 - unicode path (info-zip)
;       0x6375 - unicode comment (info-zip)
</pre>

There MUST NOT be any following bytes after this CBOR item.

TBW

<h4 id=content-block>
Content blocks</h4>

Any other block is a <dfn>content block</dfn> which has to be described by a subsequent [[#metadata-block|metadata block]].

<h5 id=compressed-block>
Compressed block</h5>

Blocks 20<sub>16</sub> through 3F<sub>16</sub> are **compressed blocks**.
Compressed blocks are parametrized by following variables:

<div dfn-for=cblock>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th><dfn>has_dictionary</dfn>
      <th><dfn>transformed</dfn>
      <th>[=compression_method=]
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>20<sub>16</sub> — 23<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>24<sub>16</sub> — 27<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>28<sub>16</sub> — 2B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>2C<sub>16</sub> — 2F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>30<sub>16</sub> — 33<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>34<sub>16</sub> — 37<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>38<sub>16</sub> — 3B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>3C<sub>16</sub> — 3F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>001|d|&thinsp;|x||c||ss|</code> where:

    * |d| indicates dictionary usage: 0 for no dictionary, 1 for dictionary present.
    * |x| indicates transformer usage: 0 for no transformer, 1 for transformer present.
    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

Its data consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>transformer_index</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=cblock/transformed=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_index</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=cblock/has_dictionary=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>chunks</dfn>
      <td>[=cblock/chunk=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

If a transformer or dictionary are in use,
respectively, 0-based [=cblock/transformer_index=] and [=cblock/dictionary_index=] are specified.
A compressed block can only use transformer and dictionary blocks
between the previous metadata block (or the beginning of the file if any) and the current compressed block,
and transformer or dictionary are numbered in the order of appearance.
These fields are intended for linear scanning,
and the metadata block contains all necessary information for random access.

A compressed block consists of one or more <dfn>chunk</dfn>s.
Each chunk can be decompressed and postprocessed without referring to any other chunk, allowing efficient random access.
Each [=cblock/chunk=] consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compressed_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size_delta</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compression_params</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=cblock/compressed_size=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None
  </tbody>
</table>

Except for [=cblock/compressed_size=] which is now explicit,
the interpretation of these fields is identical to that of the Shared compressed external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]).

TBW (add expected wasm execution profile, if possible)

</div>

<h5 id=uncompressed-block>
Uncompressed block</h5>

Blocks 40<sub>16</sub> through 47<sub>16</sub> are **uncompressed blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
where each parameter is determined as follows:

<div dfn-for=ublock>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>40<sub>16</sub> — 43<sub>16</sub>
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>44<sub>16</sub> — 47<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0100&thinsp;0|c||ss|</code> where:

    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure of an uncompressed block represents a raw, uninterpreted byte sequence.
This block type MUST be used when given file is barely compressible and can't be represented in terms of compressed blocks.

<div class=note>
  This is mandatory because each Zstandard block incurs 3 bytes of overhead
  and [=cblock/original_size_delta=] can't be negative.
</div>

</div>

<h5 id=dictionary-block>
Dictionary block</h5>

Blocks 48<sub>16</sub> through 4F<sub>16</sub> are **dictionary blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
where each parameter is determined as follows:

<div dfn-for=dict>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>[=compression_method=]
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>48<sub>16</sub> — 4B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>4C<sub>16</sub> — 4F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0100&thinsp;1|c||ss|</code> where:

    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

TBW

</div>

<h5 id=transformer-block>
Transformer block</h5>

Blocks 60<sub>16</sub> through 67<sub>16</sub> are **transformer blocks**.
Transformer blocks are parametrized by following variables:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>[=compression_method=]
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>60<sub>16</sub> — 63<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>64<sub>16</sub> — 67<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0110&thinsp;0|c||ss|</code> where:

    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=xform>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>wasm_module</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

TBW

</div>

<h4 id=reserved-blocks>
Reserved blocks</h4>

Blocks whose type's hexadecimal representation is at least 2 digits long
and starts with D<sub>16</sub> (e.g. D0<sub>16</sub> through DF<sub>16</sub>, D00<sub>16</sub> through DFF<sub>16</sub>, …)
are <dfn>reserved for development environments</dfn>.
This specification doesn't assign any meanings to these blocks.
These blocks MUST be written or read only with specific instructions to enable them.

Blocks which type's hexadecimal representation is at least 2 digits long
and starts with E<sub>16</sub> (e.g. E0<sub>16</sub> through EF<sub>16</sub>, E00<sub>16</sub> through EFF<sub>16</sub>, …)
are <dfn>reserved for experiments</dfn>.
This specification doesn't assign any meanings to those blocks.

The following block types are invalid due to the use as block-like structures.
The valid archive MUST NOT include such block types as a proper block.

* Block 278<sub>16</sub> ([=const/HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the unencrypted archive header.
* Block 98&thinsp;68EB&thinsp;D5FD<sub>16</sub> ([=const/FOOTER_TYPE=]) is used for the archive footer.
* Block 15D&thinsp;14FC&thinsp;E5F9<sub>16</sub> ([=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the encrypted archive header.

<h3 id=archive>
Archive</h3>

<h4 id=unencrypted-archive>
Unencrypted Archive</h4>

An **unencrypted archive** consists of a header, one or more blocks and a footer.

<div dfn-for=archive>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>header_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][6] = [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint_block</dfn>
      <td>[[#size-hint-block|size_hint_block]]
      <td>[=cond/Optional=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>blocks</dfn>
      <td>[[#block|block]][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][6] = [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_size</dfn>
      <td>[=u16le=] = [=archive/footer_end=] - [=archive/footer_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=archive/archive_end=] - [=archive/archive_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>metadata_backward_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_size_copy</dfn>
      <td>[=u16le=] = [=archive/footer_size=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

[=archive/header_magic=] is a fixed 6-byte sequence to identify the file as an unencrypted Aqueous archive.
This header also marks the beginning of the archive, when the archive doesn't start at the beginning of the file.

[=archive/footer_magic=] is a fixed 6-byte sequence to mark the beginning of the footer.
The footer also marks the end of the archive, when the archive doesn't end at the end of the file.
The offset between [=archive/header_magic=] and [=archive/footer_magic=] is recorded as [=archive/size_hint=],
which MAY be also replicated as [=archive/size_hint_block=] if random access is desired.

The footer size, which is the size of all fields after (but not including) [=archive/footer_magic=],
is recorded both in [=archive/footer_size=] and in [=archive/footer_size_copy=].
Implementations are therefore expected to first identify [=archive/header_magic=] and skip to the end of the file
to read [=archive/footer_size_copy=] and eventually [=archive/footer_magic=] to read the footer.

<div class=note>
  [=archive/footer_size=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=] are [=u16le=] instead of [=leb128=].
  Therefore the footer size is limited to 65,535 bytes,
  and a single read of 65,543 bytes (including [=archive/footer_magic=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=]) is
  sufficient to guarantee that the entire footer is read.
</div>

[=archive/metadata_backward_offset=] is the offset from [=archive/footer_start=] to the beginning of last metadata block,
just like [=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] is the offset from the beginning of the current metadata block to the previous one.

[=archive/blocks=] are logically divided into multiple parts,
consisting of one or more [=content blocks=] and a single [[#metadata-block|metadata block]] describing them,
with optional spacer blocks in between.
Each part can be independently processed and decompressed in the reading order.
There SHOULD NOT be multiple [=meta/entry|entries=] with the same path across metadata blocks,
and if there are, only the last one is considered valid.

Alternatively, [=archive/blocks=] can have no metadata block,
in which case there SHOULD exist exactly one of either [[#compressed-block|compressed block]] or [[#uncompressed-block|uncompressed block]].
Such archive decompresses into a single unnamed file with all content blocks concatenated.

</div>

<h4 id=encrypted-archive>
Encrypted Archive</h4>

An archive might have been **encrypted**,
in which case it consists of a single block of type [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=] followed by an encrypted binary payload.

<div dfn-for=envelope>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>encrypted_header_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][8] = [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>lg_work_factor</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] ∈ [0C<sub>16</sub>, 3F<sub>16</sub>)
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>scrypt_ephemeral_secret</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][16]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>encrypted_file_key</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>age_header_mac</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>payload</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

All fields except for [=payload=] are used to reconstruct an AGE v1 textual file header [[AGE]] as follows:

<pre>
age-encryption.org/v1
-> scrypt <var>scrypt_ephemeral_secret_base64</var> <var>lg_work_factor_decimal</var>
<var>encrypted_file_key_base64</var>
--- <var>age_header_mac_base64</var>
</pre>

Where,

* |scrypt_ephemeral_secret_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/scrypt_ephemeral_secret=] without padding ([[RFC4648]], Section 4);
* |lg_work_factor_decimal| is the decimal representation of [=envelope/lg_work_factor=] without leading zeros;
* |encrypted_file_key_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/encrypted_file_key=] without padding;
* |age_header_mac_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/age_header_mac=] without padding.

Subsequent [=payload=] contains the file nonce and encrypted chunks and interpreted in accordance to the AGE v1 format.

The encrypted payload MUST decrypt into a valid unencrypted archive ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
with the following additional constraints:

* [=archive/size_hint_block=] is no longer optional;
* No spacer block ([[#spacer-block]]) is allowed, in order to discourage an in-place modification of the encrypted archive;
* No trailing bytes are allowed after the archive.

</div>

<h2 id=semantics>
Semantics</h2>

<h3 id=exec-env>
Execution Environment</h3>

The execution environment is an opaque state that provides an interface to the external system,
primarily including the file system.

Every specified algorithm assumes that the file system doesn't change during the operation;
the behavior when such changes occur is implementation-defined.
As such, the execution environment is assumed to have an access to the <dfn>file system snapshot</dfn>,
which type is an [=ordered map=] with [=file system names=] as keys and [=file system entries=] as values.

A <dfn>file system name</dfn> is a byte sequence [=u8=][*] with the unspecified encoding, commonly derived from:

* A Unicode string, in which case the encoding is UTF-8;
* A UTF-16 string with lone surrogates (cf. NTFS), in which case the encoding is WTF-8 [[WTF-8]];
* An uninterpreted raw byte sequence, in which case the encoding is the identity function.

A <dfn>file system path</dfn> is a list of [=file system names=] representing a path from the root directory to a file or directory.

A <dfn>file system entry</dfn> is either a [=file entry=] or a [=directory entry=].

A <dfn>file entry</dfn> is a [=struct=]... contains a byte sequence of <dfn for=file-entry>data</dfn>.

A <dfn>directory entry</dfn> is a [=struct=]... contains an [=ordered map=] <dfn for=directory-entry>entries</dfn> from [=file system names=] to [=file system entries=].

To <dfn>look up a file system entry</dfn> in a [=file system snapshot=] |snapshot|, given a [=file system path=] |path|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. If the |path| is empty, return a synthesized [=directory entry=] with the |snapshot| as [=directory-entry/entries=].
    2. Let |name| be |path|[0].
    3. If |snapshot|[|name|] doesn't exist, [=throw=] an error.
    4. Let |entry| be |snapshot|[|name|].
    5. If |entry| is a [=file entry=],
        1. If the [=list/size=] of |path| is larger than 1, [=throw=] an error.
        2. Return |entry|.
    6. If |entry| is a [=directory entry=],
        1. Set |snapshot| to the [=directory-entry/entries=] of |entry|.
        2. Remove the first component from |path|.
        3. Repeat from step 1.
</ol>

An <dfn>input selector algorithm</dfn> rooted at a [=file system path=] |input root directory| is
an algorithm that takes a [=file system path=] relative to the |input root directory|
and returns a [=boolean=] indicating whether the file or directory at that path should be included in the archive.

<h3 id=compressor>
Compressor</h3>

To <dfn export>compress files</dfn> into an Aqueous archive,
given a [=file system snapshot=] |snapshot|,
a [=file system path=] |input root directory| and
an [=input selector algorithm=] |input selector| rooted at the |input root directory|.

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. 
</ol>

<!--
<p>To <dfn export>compress a file</dfn> into an Aqueous archive, given |inputPath| (a file system path), |outputPath| (a file system path), and optional |options|:
</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |inputFile| be the result of opening |inputPath| for reading.
    If |inputFile| is null, [=throw=] an error.</li>

  <li>Let |inputSize| be the size of |inputFile| in bytes.</li>

  <li>Let |outputFile| be the result of opening |outputPath| for writing.
    If |outputFile| is null, [=throw=] an error.</li>

  <li>Let |metadataBlocks| be an empty list.</li>
  <li>Let |contentBlocks| be an empty list.</li>
  <li>Let |transformerBlocks| be an empty list.</li>
  <li>Let |dictionaryBlocks| be an empty list.</li>

  <li>Let |transformerIndex| be 0.</li>
  <li>Let |dictionaryIndex| be 0.</li>

  <li>Write the [=archive/header_magic=] to |outputFile|.</li>

  <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["size_hint"] is true:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |sizeHintBlock| be a [=size hint block=] with [=szhint/size_hint=] set to 0 (placeholder).</li>
      <li>Write |sizeHintBlock| to |outputFile|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["transformer"] is provided:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |transformerData| be |options|["transformer"].</li>
      <li>Let |compressedTransformer| be the result of [=compressing a byte sequence=] with |transformerData| using the Zstandard compression method.
        If the compression fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
      <li>Let |transformerBlock| be a [=transformer block=] containing |compressedTransformer|.</li>
      <li>Append |transformerBlock| to |transformerBlocks|.</li>
      <li>Increment |transformerIndex| by 1.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["dictionary"] is provided:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |dictionaryData| be |options|["dictionary"].</li>
      <li>Let |compressedDictionary| be the result of [=compressing a byte sequence=] with |dictionaryData| using the Zstandard compression method.
        If the compression fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
      <li>Let |dictionaryBlock| be a [=dictionary block=] containing |compressedDictionary|.</li>
      <li>Append |dictionaryBlock| to |dictionaryBlocks|.</li>
      <li>Increment |dictionaryIndex| by 1.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Let |chunkSize| be the maximum chunk size.
    If |options| is provided and |options|["chunk_size"] is provided, set |chunkSize| to |options|["chunk_size"].
    Otherwise, set |chunkSize| to 2<sup>21</sup> - 1 (the maximum Zstandard block size).</li>

  <li>Let |chunkOffset| be 0.</li>
  <li>Let |chunkIndex| be 0.</li>
  <li>Let |compressedChunks| be an empty list.</li>

  <li>While |chunkOffset| is less than |inputSize|:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |remainingSize| be |inputSize| - |chunkOffset|.</li>
      <li>Let |currentChunkSize| be min(|remainingSize|, |chunkSize|).</li>

      <li>Let |chunkData| be the result of reading |currentChunkSize| bytes from |inputFile| starting at offset |chunkOffset|.
        If the read fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>

      <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["transformer"] is provided:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |transformedData| be the result of applying the transformer specified by |options|["transformer"] to |chunkData| as preprocessing.
            If the transformation fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
          <li>Set |chunkData| to |transformedData|.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Let |compressionParams| be an empty byte sequence.</li>
      <li>Let |compressedData| be an empty byte sequence.
      <li>Let |useCompression| be true.</li>

      <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["dictionary"] is provided:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |dictionary| be the dictionary data from |dictionaryBlocks|[0].</li>
          <li>Set |compressionParams| to the Zstandard [=window_descriptor=] for |dictionary|.</li>
          <li>Let |compressedData| be the result of compressing |chunkData| using Zstandard with |dictionary|.
            If the compression fails, set |useCompression| to false.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
      <li>Otherwise:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |compressedData| be the result of compressing |chunkData| using Zstandard without a dictionary.
            If the compression fails, set |useCompression| to false.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Determine whether to use compression:
        <ol>
          <li>If |useCompression| is false, continue to the next step.</li>

          <li>Let |compressedSize| be the length of |compressedData| in bytes.</li>
          <li>Let |originalSize| be the length of |chunkData| in bytes.</li>

          <li>If |compressedSize| is greater than or equal to |originalSize|:
            <ol>
              <li>Set |useCompression| to false.
              <div class=note>
                This indicates that the data is [=barely compressible=] and should be stored uncompressed.
                For Zstandard, the minimum overhead is 3 bytes per block, so if the size saving is less than 3 bytes,
                compression should not be used.
              </div>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </li>

          <li>Let |overhead| be the sum of:
            <ul>
              <li>The size of the LEB128-encoded |originalSize| - |compressedSize| ([=cblock/original_size_delta=]),</li>
              <li>The size of the LEB128-encoded |compressedSize| ([=cblock/compressed_size=]),</li>
              <li>The length of |compressionParams|,</li>
              <li>8 if checksum is enabled (|checksum_size| for XXH64),</li>
              <li>And 3 bytes for the Zstandard block header.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>

          <li>If |compressedSize| + |overhead| is greater than or equal to |originalSize|:
            <ol>
              <li>Set |useCompression| to false.</li>
            </ol>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>If |useCompression| is true:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |originalSizeDelta| be |originalSize| - |compressedSize|.</li>

          <li>Let |checksum| be an empty byte sequence.
          <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["checksum"] is true:
            <ol>
              <li>Let |checksum| be the XXH64 hash of |compressedData| with seed 0, encoded as a u64le.</li>
            </ol>
          </li>

          <li>Let |chunk| be a [=cblock/chunk=] with:
            <ul>
              <li>[=cblock/compressed_size=] = |compressedSize|,</li>
              <li>[=cblock/original_size_delta=] = |originalSizeDelta|,</li>
              <li>[=cblock/compression_params=] = |compressionParams|,</li>
              <li>[=cblock/data=] = |compressedData|,</li>
              <li>[=cblock/checksum=] = |checksum|.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>

          <li>Append |chunk| to |compressedChunks|.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
      <li>Otherwise:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |checksum| be an empty byte sequence.
          <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["checksum"] is true:
            <ol>
              <li>Let |checksum| be the XXH64 hash of |chunkData| with seed 0, encoded as a u64le.</li>
            </ol>
          </li>

          <li>Let |uncompressedBlock| be an [=uncompressed block=] containing |chunkData| and |checksum|.</li>
          <li>Write |uncompressedBlock| to |outputFile|.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Set |chunkOffset| to |chunkOffset| + |currentChunkSize|.
      <li>Increment |chunkIndex| by 1.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>If |compressedChunks| is not empty:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |blockType| be 20<sub>16</sub> (compressed block, no dictionary, no transformer, no checksum).</li>
      <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["checksum"] is true, set |blockType| to 24<sub>16</sub>.</li>
      <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["transformer"] is provided, add 8<sub>16</sub> to |blockType|.</li>
      <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["dictionary"] is provided, add 16<sub>16</sub> to |blockType|.</li>

      <li>Let |compressedBlock| be a [=compressed block=] of type |blockType| containing all chunks in |compressedChunks|.
      <li>Write |compressedBlock| to |outputFile|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Let |archiveEnd| be the current position in |outputFile|.</li>

  <li>Let |metadataBlock| be a [=metadata block=] describing the compressed and uncompressed content.</li>
  <li>Write |metadataBlock| to |outputFile|.</li>

  <li>Let |footerStart| be the current position in |outputFile|.</li>

  <li>Let |sizeHint| be |archiveEnd| - (position after [=archive/header_magic=]).</li>

  <li>Write the [=archive/footer_magic=] to |outputFile|.</li>
  <li>Write the [=archive/footer_size=] (placeholder, to be filled later) to |outputFile|.</li>
  <li>Write the [=archive/size_hint=] to |outputFile|.</li>
  <li>Write the [=archive/metadata_backward_offset=] to |outputFile|.</li>
  <li>Let |footerEnd| be the current position in |outputFile|.</li>
  <li>Let |footerSize| be |footerEnd| - |footerStart|.</li>

  <li>Seek back to the position of [=archive/footer_size=] in |outputFile| and write |footerSize|.</li>
  <li>Write the [=archive/footer_size_copy=] (= |footerSize|) to |outputFile|.</li>

  <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["size_hint"] is true:
    <ol>
      <li>Seek back to the [=szhint/size_hint=] field in the size hint block and write |sizeHint|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Close |inputFile| and |outputFile|.</li>
  <li>Return success.</li>
</ol>

<p>To <dfn export>compress a byte sequence</dfn> given |data| and |compressionMethod|:</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>If |compressionMethod| is Zstandard:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |compressed| be the result of compressing |data| using the Zstandard compression algorithm [[RFC8878]].
        If the compression fails, return failure.</li>
      <li>Return |compressed|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Return failure.</li>
</ol>
-->

<h3 id=decompressor>
Decompressor</h3>

<!--
<p>To <dfn export>decompress an archive</dfn> given |inputPath| (a file system path), |outputPath| (a file system path), and optional |options|:</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |inputFile| be the result of opening |inputPath| for reading.
    If |inputFile| is null, [=throw=] an error.</li>

  <li>Let |inputSize| be the size of |inputFile| in bytes.</li>

  <li>Identify the archive type:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |header| be the first 8 bytes of |inputFile|.</li>

      <li>If |header| starts with [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=]:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |decryptedArchive| be the result of [=decrypting an encrypted archive=] given |inputFile|.
            If decryption fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
          <li>Set |inputFile| to |decryptedArchive|.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Otherwise, if |header| does not start with [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=]:
        <ol>
          <li>[=throw=] an error ("invalid archive format").</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Locate the footer:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |maxFooterSize| be 65,543 (maximum footer size including magic and size copy).</li>
      <li>Let |readSize| be min(|inputSize|, |maxFooterSize|).</li>

      <li>Seek to position |inputSize| - |readSize| in |inputFile|.</li>
      <li>Let |trailer| be the result of reading |readSize| bytes from |inputFile|.</li>

      <li>Let |footerMagicOffset| be the index of [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=] within |trailer|, searching from the end.
        If |footerMagicOffset| is not found, [=throw=] an error ("footer not found").</li>

      <li>Let |footerOffset| be |inputSize| - |readSize| + |footerMagicOffset|.</li>

      <li>Seek to position |footerOffset| + 6 (after [=archive/footer_magic=]) in |inputFile|.</li>
      <li>Let |footerSize| be the result of reading a u16le from |inputFile|.</li>

      <li>Seek to position |footerOffset| + 6 + 2 + |footerSize| in |inputFile|.</li>
      <li>Let |footerSizeCopy| be the result of reading a u16le from |inputFile|.</li>

      <li>If |footerSize| is not equal to |footerSizeCopy|:
        <ol>
          <li>[=throw=] an error ("footer size mismatch").</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Let |footerStart| be |footerOffset| + 6.</li>
      <li>Let |footerEnd| be |footerStart| + |footerSize|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Parse the footer:
    <ol>
      <li>Seek to position |footerStart| in |inputFile|.</li>

      <li>Let |sizeHint| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.</li>
      <li>Let |metadataBackwardOffset| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.</li>

      <li>Let |lastMetadataOffset| be |footerStart| - |metadataBackwardOffset|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Read metadata blocks:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |allMetadata| be an empty list.</li>
      <li>Let |currentMetadataOffset| be |lastMetadataOffset|.</li>

      <li>While |currentMetadataOffset| is greater than the position after [=archive/header_magic=]:
        <ol>
          <li>Seek to position |currentMetadataOffset| in |inputFile|.</li>

          <li>Let |blockType| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.
            If |blockType| is not in the range [10<sub>16</sub>, 1F<sub>16</sub>], [=throw=] an error ("invalid metadata block").</li>

          <li>Let |blockSize| be the result of reading the size field for |blockType| (u8, u16le, u32le, or u64le based on |blockType| % 4).</li>

          <li>Let |blockData| be the result of reading |blockSize| bytes from |inputFile|.</li>

          <li>Let |metadataBlock| be the result of [=parsing a metadata block=] from |blockData| with |blockType|.
            If parsing fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>

          <li>Prepend |metadataBlock| to |allMetadata|.</li>

          <li>Let |previousMetadataOffset| be |metadataBlock|.[=meta/previous_metadata_offset=].</li>
          <li>If |previousMetadataOffset| is 0:
            <ol>
              <li>Set |currentMetadataOffset| to 0.</li>
            </ol>
          </li>
          <li>Otherwise:
            <ol>
              <li>Set |currentMetadataOffset| to |currentMetadataOffset| - |previousMetadataOffset|.</li>
            </ol>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>If |allMetadata| is empty:
    <ol>
      <li>Handle single-file archive (no metadata):
        <ol>
        <li>Seek to position after [=archive/header_magic=] in |inputFile|.</li>

        <li>Let |outputFile| be the result of opening |outputPath| for writing.
          If |outputFile| is null, [=throw=] an error.</li>

        <li>Let |fileOffset| be the current position in |inputFile|.</li>

        <li>While |fileOffset| is less than |footerStart|:
          <ol>
            <li>Let |blockType| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.</li>

            <li>If |blockType| is in the range [20<sub>16</sub>, 3F<sub>16</sub>]:
              <ol>
                <li>Let |decompressedData| be the result of [=decompressing a compressed block=] given |inputFile|, |blockType|, and |options|.
                  If decompression fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
                <li>Write |decompressedData| to |outputFile|.</li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li>Else if |blockType| is in the range [40<sub>16</sub>, 47<sub>16</sub>]:
              <ol>
                <li>Let |decompressedData| be the result of [=decompressing an uncompressed block=] given |inputFile|, |blockType|, and |options|.
                  If decompression fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
                <li>Write |decompressedData| to |outputFile|.</li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li>Else if |blockType| is in the range [0<sub>16</sub>, 3<sub>16</sub>]:
              <ol>
                <li>Let |blockSize| be the result of reading the size field for |blockType|.</li>
                <li>Skip |blockSize| + 1 bytes in |inputFile| (including padding).</li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li>Otherwise:
              <ol>
                <li>[=throw=] an error ("unexpected block type").</li>
              </ol>
            </li>

            <li>Set |fileOffset| to the current position in |inputFile|.</li>
          </ol>
        </li>
        </ol>

        <li>Close |outputFile|.</li>
        <li>Return success.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Handle multi-file archive (with metadata):
    <ol>
      <li>For each |metadataBlock| in |allMetadata|:
        <ol>
          <li>For each |entry| in |metadataBlock|.[=meta/entries=]:
            <ol>
              <li>Let |entryIndex| be the index of |entry| within |metadataBlock|.[=meta/entries=].</li>
              <li>Let |entryName| be |entry|.[=meta/entry_name=].</li>

              <li>If |entryIndex| is less than or equal to |metadataBlock|.[=meta/num_directories=]:
                <ol>
                  <li>Let |dirPath| be |outputPath| joined with |entryName| using the appropriate path separator for the platform.</li>
                  <li>Create the directory at |dirPath| if it does not exist.
                    If directory creation fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
                </ol>
              </li>
              <li>Otherwise:
                <ol>
                  <li>Let |fileIndex| be |entryIndex| - |metadataBlock|.[=meta/num_directories=] - 1.</li>
                  <li>Let |fileParam| be |metadataBlock|.[=meta/file_params=][|fileIndex|].</li>

                  <li>Let |filePath| be |outputPath| joined with |entryName| using the appropriate path separator for the platform.</li>

                  <li>Let |contentBlockOffset| be |metadataBlock| offset from the beginning of |inputFile| minus |fileParam|.[=meta/file_offset=].</li>

                  <li>Seek to position |contentBlockOffset| in |inputFile|.</li>
                  <li>Let |blockType| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.</li>

                  <li>Let |fileData| be an empty byte sequence.</li>

                  <li>If |blockType| is in the range [20<sub>16</sub>, 3F<sub>16</sub>]:
                    <ol>
                      <li>Let |fileData| be the result of [=decompressing a compressed block=] given |inputFile|, |blockType|, and |options|.
                        If decompression fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
                    </ol>
                  </li>
                  <li>Else if |blockType| is in the range [40<sub>16</sub>, 47<sub>16</sub>]:
                    <ol>
                      <li>Let |fileData| be the result of [=decompressing an uncompressed block=] given |inputFile|, |blockType|, and |options|.
                        If decompression fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
                    </ol>
                  </li>
                  <li>Otherwise:
                    <ol>
                      <li>[=throw=] an error ("invalid content block type").</li>
                    </ol>
                  </li>

                  <li>Let |outputFile| be the result of opening |filePath| for writing.
                    If |outputFile| is null, [=throw=] an error.</li>

                  <li>Write |fileData| to |outputFile|.</li>
                  <li>Close |outputFile|.</li>
                </ol>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </ol>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Close |inputFile|.</li>
  <li>Return success.</li>
</ol>

<p>To <dfn export>parse a metadata block</dfn> from |blockData| with |blockType|:</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |compressionMethod| be None if |blockType| is in [10<sub>16</sub>, 17<sub>16</sub>], otherwise Zstandard.</li>
  <li>Let |checksumMethod| be None if (|blockType| >> 2) & 1 = 0, otherwise XXH64.</li>
  <li>Let |checksumSize| be 0 if |checksumMethod| is None, otherwise 8.</li>

  <li>Let |decompressedData| be |blockData|.</li>

  <li>If |compressionMethod| is Zstandard:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |originalSizeDelta| be the result of reading a LEB128 from the beginning of |blockData|.
        Let |pos| be the number of bytes consumed.</li>

      <li>Let |compressionParams| be an empty byte sequence.
        While |pos| is less than the length of |blockData| and the byte at |blockData|[|pos|] is not the start of the Zstandard data:
          <ol>
            <li>Append the byte at |blockData|[|pos|] to |compressionParams|.</li>
            <li>Increment |pos| by 1.</li>
          </ol>
        </ol>

      <li>Let |dataStart| be |pos|.</li>
      <li>Let |compressedSize| be the length of |blockData| - |dataStart| - |checksumSize|.</li>
      <li>Let |originalSize| be |compressedSize| + |originalSizeDelta|.</li>

      <li>If |checksumMethod| is XXH64:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |checksumOffset| be |dataStart| + |compressedSize|.</li>
          <li>Let |storedChecksum| be |blockData| from |checksumOffset| to |checksumOffset| + 8.</li>
          <li>Let |computedChecksum| be the XXH64 hash of |blockData| from |dataStart| to |dataStart| + |compressedSize| with seed 0, encoded as u64le.</li>
          <li>If |storedChecksum| is not equal to |computedChecksum|:
            <ol>
              <li>Return failure ("checksum mismatch").</li>
            </ol>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Let |compressedData| be |blockData| from |dataStart| to |dataStart| + |compressedSize|.</li>

      <li>Let |decompressedData| be the result of decompressing |compressedData| using Zstandard [[RFC8878]].
        The decompressed data should be a sequence of Zstandard blocks.
        Process Zstandard blocks consecutively until the final block is signalled, at which point all compressed bytes should have been consumed.
        If decompression fails or the decompressed size is not equal to |originalSize|, return failure ("decompression failed").</li>
    </ol>
  </li>
  <li>Otherwise:
    <ol>
      <li>If |checksumMethod| is XXH64:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |dataSize| be the length of |blockData| - 8.</li>
          <li>Let |storedChecksum| be |blockData| from |dataSize| to |dataSize| + 8.</li>
          <li>Let |computedChecksum| be the XXH64 hash of |blockData| from 0 to |dataSize| with seed 0, encoded as u64le.</li>
          <li>If |storedChecksum| is not equal to |computedChecksum|:
            <ol>
              <li>Return failure ("checksum mismatch").</li>
            </ol>
          </li>
          <li>Set |decompressedData| to |blockData| from 0 to |dataSize|.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Parse |decompressedData| according to the [=metadata block=] structure and return the resulting metadata object.
    If parsing fails, return failure ("invalid metadata").</li>
</ol>

<p>To <dfn export>decompress a compressed block</dfn> given |inputFile|, |blockType|, and |options|:</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |hasDictionary| be true if (|blockType| >> 5) & 1 = 1, otherwise false.</li>
  <li>Let |transformed| be true if (|blockType| >> 4) & 1 = 1, otherwise false.</li>
  <li>Let |checksumMethod| be None if (|blockType| >> 3) & 1 = 0, otherwise XXH64.</li>
  <li>Let |checksumSize| be 0 if |checksumMethod| is None, otherwise 8.</li>

  <li>Let |transformerIndex| be 0.
  <li>If |transformed| is true:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |transformerIndex| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Let |dictionaryIndex| be 0.
  <li>If |hasDictionary| is true:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |dictionaryIndex| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Let |allChunks| be an empty list.</li>

  <li>While the end of the block has not been reached:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |compressedSize| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.</li>
      <li>Let |originalSizeDelta| be the result of reading a LEB128 from |inputFile|.</li>
      <li>Let |originalSize| be |compressedSize| + |originalSizeDelta|.</li>

      <li>If |originalSize| is greater than |compressedSize|:
        <ol>
          <li>Return failure ("invalid size delta: original size cannot be larger than compressed size").</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Let |compressionParams| be an empty byte sequence.
        While the current byte is not the start of the Zstandard data:
          <ol>
            <li>Read a byte from |inputFile| and append it to |compressionParams|.</li>
          </ol>
        </ol>

      <li>Let |compressedData| be the result of reading |compressedSize| bytes from |inputFile|.</li>

      <li>Let |checksum| be an empty byte sequence.
      <li>If |checksumMethod| is XXH64:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |checksum| be the result of reading 8 bytes from |inputFile|.</li>
          <li>Let |computedChecksum| be the XXH64 hash of |compressedData| with seed 0, encoded as u64le.</li>
          <li>If |checksum| is not equal to |computedChecksum|:
            <ol>
              <li>Return failure ("checksum mismatch").</li>
            </ol>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Let |decompressedData| be the result of decompressing |compressedData| using Zstandard [[RFC8878]].
        The compressed data is a sequence of Zstandard blocks.
        Process Zstandard blocks consecutively until the final block is signalled, at which point all compressed bytes should have been consumed.
        If decompression fails or the decompressed size is not equal to |originalSize|, return failure ("decompression failed").</li>

      <li>If |transformed| is true and |options| is provided and |options|["transformer"] is provided:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |transformedData| be the result of applying the transformer specified by |transformerIndex| to |decompressedData| as postprocessing.
            If the transformation fails, return failure ("transformation failed").</li>
          <li>Set |decompressedData| to |transformedData|.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Append |decompressedData| to |allChunks|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Return the concatenation of all byte sequences in |allChunks|.</li>
</ol>

<p>To <dfn export>decompress an uncompressed block</dfn> given |inputFile|, |blockType|, and |options|:</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |checksumMethod| be None if (|blockType| >> 2) & 1 = 0, otherwise XXH64.</li>
  <li>Let |checksumSize| be 0 if |checksumMethod| is None, otherwise 8.</li>

  <li>Let |blockSize| be the result of reading the size field for |blockType| (u8, u16le, u32le, or u64le based on |blockType| % 4).</li>

  <li>Let |dataSize| be |blockSize| - |checksumSize|.</li>

  <li>Let |data| be the result of reading |dataSize| bytes from |inputFile|.</li>

  <li>If |checksumMethod| is XXH64:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |checksum| be the result of reading 8 bytes from |inputFile|.</li>
      <li>Let |computedChecksum| be the XXH64 hash of |data| with seed 0, encoded as u64le.</li>
      <li>If |checksum| is not equal to |computedChecksum|:
        <ol>
          <li>Return failure ("checksum mismatch").</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Skip the padding byte if present (if the total block size including the padding byte is odd).</li>

  <li>Return |data|.</li>
</ol>

<p>To <dfn export>decrypt an encrypted archive</dfn> given |inputFile|:</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |encryptedHeaderMagic| be the result of reading 8 bytes from |inputFile|.
    If |encryptedHeaderMagic| is not equal to [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=], return failure ("invalid encrypted header").</li>

  <li>Let |lgWorkFactor| be the result of reading 1 byte from |inputFile|.</li>
  <li>If |lgWorkFactor| is not in the range [0C<sub>16</sub>, 3F<sub>16</sub>), return failure ("invalid work factor").</li>

  <li>Let |scryptEphemeralSecret| be the result of reading 16 bytes from |inputFile|.</li>
  <li>Let |encryptedFileKey| be the result of reading 32 bytes from |inputFile|.</li>
  <li>Let |ageHeaderMac| be the result of reading 32 bytes from |inputFile|.</li>

  <li>Let |payload| be the remaining bytes in |inputFile|.</li>

  <li>Construct the AGE v1 header:
    <pre>
age-encryption.org/v1
-> scrypt <var>scryptEphemeralSecretBase64</var> <var>lgWorkFactorDecimal</var>
<var>encryptedFileKeyBase64</var>
--- <var>ageHeaderMacBase64</var>
    </pre>
    Where:
    <ul>
      <li>|scryptEphemeralSecretBase64| is the base64 encoding of |scryptEphemeralSecret| without padding.</li>
      <li>|lgWorkFactorDecimal| is the decimal representation of |lgWorkFactor|.</li>
      <li>|encryptedFileKeyBase64| is the base64 encoding of |encryptedFileKey| without padding.</li>
      <li>|ageHeaderMacBase64| is the base64 encoding of |ageHeaderMac| without padding.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>

  <li>Prompt the user for the decryption passphrase.</li>

  <li>Let |decryptedPayload| be the result of decrypting |payload| using the AGE v1 format [[AGE]] with the provided passphrase.
    If decryption fails, return failure ("decryption failed").</li>

  <li>Verify that |decryptedPayload| is a valid unencrypted archive:
    <ol>
      <li>If |decryptedPayload| does not start with [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=], return failure ("invalid decrypted archive").</li>
      <li>If |decryptedPayload| does not contain a [=archive/size_hint_block=], return failure ("missing size hint block").</li>
      <li>If |decryptedPayload| contains any spacer blocks, return failure ("unexpected spacer block").</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Return |decryptedPayload| as a byte sequence.</li>
</ol>
-->

<h3 id=transformer>
Transformer</h3>

<!--
<p>A <dfn>transformer</dfn> is a WebAssembly module that performs preprocessing on data before compression and postprocessing after decompression.
Transformers are identified by a 1-based index within the metadata block.</p>

<p>Transformers are stored in [=transformer blocks=] and can be referenced by [=compressed blocks=] and [=file parameters=].</p>

<p>To <dfn export>apply a transformer</dfn> given |transformerModule| (a WebAssembly module), |inputData| (a byte sequence), and |mode| ("preprocess" or "postprocess"):</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |instance| be the result of instantiating |transformerModule| as a WebAssembly module.
    If instantiation fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>

  <li>If |instance| does not export an exported function named "transform":
    <ol>
      <li>[=throw=] an error ("transformer missing required export").</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Let |transformFunction| be the exported "transform" function from |instance|.</li>

  <li>Let |memory| be the exported memory from |instance|, if any.
    Otherwise, let |memory| be the default WebAssembly memory.</li>

  <li>Let |inputOffset| be the result of allocating |inputData|.[=length=] bytes in |memory|.
    If allocation fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>

  <li>Copy |inputData| into |memory| at offset |inputOffset|.</li>

  <li>Let |modeValue| be 1 if |mode| is "preprocess", otherwise 2 if |mode| is "postprocess".</li>

  <li>Let |result| be the result of invoking |transformFunction| with arguments (|inputOffset|, |inputData|.[=length=], |modeValue|).
    If the invocation fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>

  <li>If |result| is not a WebAssembly integer or is negative:
    <ol>
      <li>[=throw=] an error ("transformer returned invalid result").</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Let |outputOffset| be |result|.</li>

  <li>If |instance| exports an exported function named "get_output_size":
    <ol>
      <li>Let |getOutputSizeFunction| be the exported "get_output_size" function from |instance|.</li>
      <li>Let |outputSize| be the result of invoking |getOutputSizeFunction| with no arguments.</li>
      <li>If |outputSize| is not a WebAssembly integer or is negative:
        <ol>
          <li>[=throw=] an error ("transformer returned invalid output size").</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </li>
  <li>Otherwise:
    <ol>
      <li>[=throw=] an error ("transformer missing get_output_size export").</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Let |outputData| be the result of reading |outputSize| bytes from |memory| at offset |outputOffset|.</li>

  <li>If |instance| exports an exported function named "cleanup":
    <ol>
      <li>Let |cleanupFunction| be the exported "cleanup" function from |instance|.</li>
      <li>Invoke |cleanupFunction| with no arguments. Ignore any errors.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Return |outputData|.</li>
</ol>

<p class=note>Transformers SHOULD be deterministic: the same input with the same mode SHOULD always produce the same output.
Non-deterministic transformers MAY produce different outputs for the same input, but this MAY result in different compressed archives for the same uncompressed data.</p>

<p class=note>Transformers SHOULD NOT have side effects external to the WebAssembly module (such as file system or network access).
Implementations MAY restrict or prohibit such side effects.</p>

<p>To <dfn export>validate a transformer module</dfn> given |transformerModule|:</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |instance| be the result of instantiating |transformerModule| as a WebAssembly module.
    If instantiation fails, return failure.</li>

  <li>If |instance| does not export an exported function named "transform", return failure.</li>

  <li>If |instance| does not export an exported function named "get_output_size", return failure.</li>

  <li>Return success.</li>
</ol>

<p>The following interface is the expected interface for transformer modules:</p>

<pre class=def>
// Exported functions:
export function transform(input_offset: number, input_size: number, mode: number): number;
// Returns the offset in WebAssembly memory where the output data is written.
// mode: 1 for preprocessing (before compression), 2 for postprocessing (after decompression)

export function get_output_size(): number;
// Returns the size of the output data in bytes.

export function cleanup(): void;
// Optional. Called after transform() to release any resources.

// WebAssembly memory:
export memory: WebAssembly.Memory;
// Optional. If not provided, the default memory is used.
</pre>

<div class=note>
  <p>Transformer execution profile:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Transformers SHOULD execute in O(n) time where n is the input size.</li>
    <li>Transformers SHOULD use O(1) additional memory beyond the input and output buffers.</li>
    <li>Transformers SHOULD complete within a reasonable time frame (e.g., less than 1 second per MB of data on typical hardware).</li>
    <li>Implementations MAY impose resource limits on transformer execution (time, memory, CPU cycles).</li>
  </ul>
</div>
-->

<h2 id=conformance>
Conformance</h2>

<!--
<p>As well as sections marked as non-normative, all authoring guidelines, diagrams, examples, and notes in this specification are non-normative.
Everything else in this specification is normative.</p>

<p>The key words MAY, MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, and SHOULD NOT in this specification are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [[RFC2119]]
when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.</p>

<p>A conforming <dfn>compressor</dfn> MUST implement all algorithms specified in this specification.
Specifically:</p>

<ul>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=compress a file=] algorithm.</li>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=compress a byte sequence=] algorithm.</li>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=apply a transformer=] algorithm for preprocessing.</li>
  <li>It MUST generate valid archives that conform to the syntax specified in the Syntax section.</li>
  <li>It MUST handle [=barely compressible=] data correctly by using uncompressed blocks when compression would not reduce size.</li>
  <li>It MUST generate valid checksums when checksums are enabled.</li>
  <li>It MUST ensure that all constraints specified in the Syntax section are satisfied (e.g., offset ranges, size limits, etc.).</li>
</ul>

<p>A conforming <dfn>decompressor</dfn> MUST implement all algorithms specified in this specification.
Specifically:</p>

<ul>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=decompress an archive=] algorithm.</li>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=parse a metadata block=] algorithm.</li>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=decompress a compressed block=] algorithm.</li>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=decompress an uncompressed block=] algorithm.</li>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=decrypt an encrypted archive=] algorithm if it supports encrypted archives.</li>
  <li>It MUST correctly implement the [=apply a transformer=] algorithm for postprocessing.</li>
  <li>It MUST validate checksums when present and fail on checksum mismatch.</li>
  <li>It MUST validate all constraints specified in the Syntax section and fail on constraint violations.</li>
</ul>

<p>A conforming <dfn>transformer</dfn> (WebAssembly module) MUST implement the required interface:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>It MUST export a function named "transform" that accepts the specified parameters.</li>
  <li>It MUST export a function named "get_output_size" that returns the output size.</li>
  <li>It MUST write output data to WebAssembly memory at the offset returned by "transform".</li>
  <li>It MAY export a function named "cleanup" for resource cleanup.</li>
</ul>

<p class=note>Conformance for transformers is defined at the module interface level.
The actual transformation logic is implementation-dependent and not specified by this standard.</p>

<p>An implementation is not required to support:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Encryption/decryption (encrypted archives).</li>
  <li>Transformer blocks and transformation.</li>
  <li>Dictionary blocks and dictionary-based compression.</li>
  <li>Optional metadata (CBOR-encoded).</li>
  <li>Specific compression levels or Zstandard features beyond the basic frame format.</li>
</ul>

<p>However, if an implementation claims to support any of these optional features, it MUST conform to the relevant algorithms and constraints specified in this specification.</p>

<p>Implementations MAY impose additional restrictions beyond those specified in this specification:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Maximum file size or archive size limits.</li>
  <li>Maximum number of files or directories in an archive.</li>
  <li>Maximum chunk size or number of chunks.</li>
  <li>Resource limits for transformer execution (time, memory, CPU).</li>
  <li>Restrictions on supported file names, metadata, or path separators.</li>
</ul>

<p>Such restrictions SHOULD be documented and SHOULD NOT prevent processing of valid archives that do not exceed the specified limits.</p>

<p>A conforming implementation MAY:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Reject archives that exceed implementation-defined limits.</li>
  <li>Use optimized or alternative algorithms that produce the same result as the specified algorithms.</li>
  <li>Perform operations in a different order than specified, as long as the result is identical.</li>
  <li>Cache intermediate results for performance.</li>
  <li>Parallelize independent operations (e.g., decompressing multiple chunks concurrently).</li>
</ul>

<p>A conforming implementation MUST NOT:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Accept invalid archives or archives that violate the specified syntax.</li>
  <li>Generate invalid archives or archives that violate the specified syntax.</li>
  <li>Ignore checksums when present.</li>
  <li>Ignore constraint violations (e.g., size limits, offset ranges).</li>
  <li>Fail to process a valid archive that does not exceed implementation-defined limits.</li>
</ul>
-->

<h2 id=block-index>
Block index</h2>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>Definition
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>00<sub>16</sub> — 03<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#spacer-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>04<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#size-hint-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>05<sub>16</sub> — 0F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>Reserved
    <tr>
      <td>10<sub>16</sub> — 1F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#metadata-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>20<sub>16</sub> — 3F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#compressed-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>40<sub>16</sub> — 47<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#uncompressed-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>48<sub>16</sub> — 4F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#dictionary-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>50<sub>16</sub> — 5F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>Reserved
    <tr>
      <td>60<sub>16</sub> — 67<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#transformer-block]]
    <tr>
      <td>68<sub>16</sub> — CF<sub>16</sub>
      <td>Reserved
    <tr>
      <td>D0<sub>16</sub> — DF<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[=Reserved for development environments=]
    <tr>
      <td>E0<sub>16</sub> — EF<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[=Reserved for experiments=]
    <tr>
      <td>F0<sub>16</sub> — FF<sub>16</sub>
      <td>Reserved
    <tr>
      <td>100<sub>16</sub> — FFFF&thinsp;FFFF&thinsp;FFFF&thinsp;FFFF<sub>16</sub>
      <td>Reserved for various purposes
  </tbody>
</table>

